{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e22f4082-815d-4291-8ec0-471a24337109","agentId":"a348f3ee234ac4fb3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Build the file `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/newton.py` — the Newton continuation module for gates 5 and 7 of the M8.5-C2 qualification protocol.\n\n## Context\nThis is part of the M8.5-C2 Build Unit commission. The module implements Newton's method for finding standing-wave solutions and the scored observable computations.\n\n## Dependencies already built:\n- `build/group.py` — `pi_n_unitary()`, `quat_to_su2()`, `ALL_REPS`, `multiplicity()`, `DIMS`\n- `build/sections.py` — `build_basis_object()`, `total_modes()`\n- `build/operators.py` — `laplacian_eigenvalues()`, `linear_operator()`, `real_jacobian()`, `K_defect()`, `J_defect()`\n- `build/quadrature.py` — `production_rule()`, `synthesis()`, `analysis()`\n- `build/galerkin.py` — `project_cubic()` for P_N[|ψ|²ψ]\n\n## What to implement:\n\n### 1. Residual computation\n```python\ndef residual(phi, omega, rho, N, c1=1.0, basis_obj=None, forcing=None):\n    \"\"\"Compute R(φ; ω) = Δφ − c1⟨φ,φ⟩φ + ω²φ in Galerkin coordinates.\n    \n    - Δφ: multiply each coefficient by -n(n+2) (the Laplacian eigenvalue)\n    - c1⟨φ,φ⟩φ: project_cubic from galerkin.py\n    - ω²φ: just ω² times the coefficient vector\n    - forcing: optional forcing G(s) added to residual (for Control A)\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\nThe Laplacian is diagonal: entry i gets eigenvalue -n_i(n_i+2) where n_i is the level of mode i. Use `laplacian_eigenvalues(rho, N)` from operators.py.\n\n### 2. Newton solver (unbordered, for Control A)\n```python\ndef newton_solve_unbordered(phi0, omega, rho, N, c1=1.0, basis_obj=None,\n                            forcing=None, lambda_scale=8.0, max_iter=30):\n    \"\"\"Newton's method on the square system R(φ; ω) = 0 at fixed ω.\n    \n    Stopping: ||R||₂ ≤ 1e-12 · lambda_scale · ||φ||₂\n    Hard cap: max_iter iterations\n    Returns: (phi_converged, converged_flag, history)\n    \n    The Jacobian is the real Jacobian on R^{2m}:\n    J = diag(-n(n+2)) + ω²I - c1||φ||²I - 2c1 v vᵀ\n    where v = [Re(φ); Im(φ)]\n    \n    Newton step: solve J @ δv = -r in R^{2m}, then update φ += δφ\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\n### 3. Newton solver (bordered, for Control B)\n```python\ndef newton_solve_bordered(phi0, omega0, a_target, rho, N, c1=1.0,\n                          basis_obj=None, z_tangents=None, max_iter=30,\n                          lambda_l=168.0):\n    \"\"\"Newton's method on the bordered system.\n    \n    The bordered system has:\n    - Field rows: R(φ; ω)\n    - Amplitude row: ||φ||₂ - a\n    - Border rows: c_k(φ) = ⟨ẑ_k, φ - φ_prev⟩ for each predicted continuous zero\n    Border columns: the orbit-tangent vectors ẑ_k\n    \n    Converged when ∞-norm of the scaled augmented residual ≤ 1e-12.\n    Scaled augmented residual:\n    - Field rows: R / (λ_l ||φ||₂)\n    - Amplitude row: (||φ||₂ - a) / a\n    - Border rows: c_k / ||φ||₂\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\n### 4. Control A setup\n```python\ndef control_a_setup():\n    \"\"\"Set up Control A: the manufactured two-level {2,6} full-S³ system.\n    \n    Returns the basis_obj, forcing function G(s), and reference state φ*(s).\n    \n    φ*(s) = s * v₂ + (s²/2) * v₆\n    where v₂, v₆ are the first canonical basis vectors of levels 2 and 6.\n    ω*² = λ₂ + 1 = 9 (so ω* = 3)\n    λ_scale,A = λ₂ = 8\n    \n    The Control A system uses FULL S³ scalar harmonics at levels {2,6}.\n    Level 2 has 3 modes (dimension 3 of V_2)\n    Level 6 has 7 modes (dimension 7 of V_6) \n    Total: 10 complex modes = 20 real dimensions\n    Wait - this is full S³, not 2I-restricted. So the \"multiplicity\" at each level is just 1\n    (the full representation), and the modes per level are (n+1)² from all m,m' values...\n    \n    Actually re-reading: level n on full S³ has dimension (n+1)² (all the matrix elements of\n    the spin-n/2 representation). But the protocol says \"58 complex modes\": level 2 gives\n    (2+1)² = 9 modes, level 6 gives (6+1)² = 49 modes, total 9+49 = 58. Yes.\n    \n    For full S³ scalar harmonics, the basis functions are the matrix elements of the\n    irreducible representations of SU(2). At level n, they are D^n_{m,m'} for \n    m,m' = -n/2, ..., n/2, giving (n+1)² functions.\n    \n    In our unitarized notation, the basis at level n is π_n(x)_{a,b} for a,b = 0,...,n,\n    and the orthonormality relation is:\n    ∫_{S³} π_n(x)_{a,b}^* π_m(x)_{c,d} dx = δ_{nm} δ_{ac} δ_{bd} / (n+1)\n    \n    So the orthonormal basis functions are √(n+1) · π_n(x)_{a,b}.\n    \n    The coefficient vector for Control A has 58 entries, one per (a,b) pair at each level.\n    \n    For the implementation, the Laplacian on the full-S³ system assigns eigenvalue n(n+2) to \n    every mode at level n.\n    \n    Seed: φ_seed = φ*(s) + 1e-3 · ||φ*(s)||₂ · v_{2,2}\n    where v_{2,2} is the SECOND canonical basis vector of level 2.\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\n### 5. Scored observables (§ 5 definitions)\n```python\ndef scored_observables(phi, omega, rho, N, c1=1.0, basis_obj=None,\n                       z_prev_span=None, free_level=None):\n    \"\"\"Compute all scored quantities at a converged state.\n    \n    On the UNBORDERED real Jacobian restricted to Z(φ)⊥:\n    \n    1. Zero-count gate (§ 5):\n       - MEASURED count by Sylvester inertia\n       - PREDICTED count: rank_R Z(φ), Z(φ) = span_R{iφ, T₁φ, T₂φ, T₃φ}\n       \n    2. Cluster identification (first point: smallest |λ|; subsequent: continuity tracking)\n    \n    3. Cluster position = mean of cluster eigenvalues\n    \n    4. Cluster splitting = max - min of cluster eigenvalues\n    \n    5. Leakage = sin of largest canonical angle between cluster span and F⊥\n    \n    Returns dict with all scored quantities.\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\n### 6. Right-SU(2) generators\n```python\ndef right_generators(rho, N, basis_obj):\n    \"\"\"Build the three right-SU(2) generators T₁, T₂, T₃ in the Galerkin basis.\n    \n    The right action on π_n(x) is π_n(x·g), which in the Galerkin basis at level n\n    acts as π_n(x) → π_n(x) · g, i.e., right multiplication by g on the (n+1)×(n+1)\n    matrix. In coordinates, this is the action on the second (column) index.\n    \n    For the generators of SU(2):\n    T_a acts on V_n^* via the standard spin-n/2 representation on the column index.\n    \n    J₊ v(m) = √((j-m)(j+m+1)) v(m+1)  where j = n/2\n    J₋ = J₊ᵀ\n    J₃ = diag(m)\n    T₁ = (J₊ - J₋)/2\n    T₂ = i(J₊ + J₋)/2  \n    T₃ = i J₃\n    \n    In the Galerkin basis with intertwiners, we need to express these generators.\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\n### 7. Noether identity check (gate 5)\n```python\ndef noether_check(phi, phi_dot, rho, N, c1=1.0, basis_obj=None):\n    \"\"\"Check Noether identities: |⟨∇I, F⟩| / (||∇I||₂ ||F||₂) ≤ 1e-12\n    \n    for I ∈ {E, Q, M₁, M₂, M₃} on the phase-space state (ψ, ψ̇).\n    \n    F is the time derivative operator (the right-hand side of the equations of motion).\n    ∇I is the gradient of the conserved quantity.\n    \n    Energy E = (1/2)||ψ̇||² + (1/2)⟨ψ, -Δψ⟩ + (c1/4)||ψ||⁴\n    Charge Q = Im⟨ψ̇, ψ⟩\n    Momenta M_a = Re⟨ψ̇, T_a ψ⟩\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\nRead the existing modules first:\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/operators.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/galerkin.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/group.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/sections.py`\n- `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/quadrature.py`\n\nFor Control A's full-S³ system, you may need to build a separate simple basis that doesn't use the 2I intertwiners — just the raw matrix elements √(n+1) π_n(x)_{a,b} as basis functions. The Laplacian is still diagonal with eigenvalue n(n+2) at level n.\n\nWrite the file and test that:\n1. The residual at a constructed solution is near zero\n2. Newton converges from a nearby initial guess\n3. Control A returns to the manufactured solution from the perturbed seed"},"uuid":"7134a5e4-c6cc-4bd8-8acd-7617fce416aa","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:41:25.623Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"7134a5e4-c6cc-4bd8-8acd-7617fce416aa","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a348f3ee234ac4fb3","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_delta","addedNames":["EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","Monitor","NotebookEdit","SendMessage","TaskStop","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__complete_authentication"],"addedLines":["EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","Monitor","NotebookEdit","SendMessage","TaskStop","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__complete_authentication"],"removedNames":[],"wireHiddenNames":[],"readdedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"db7dee1a-955b-4383-afec-afcfb987459a","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:41:25.627Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"db7dee1a-955b-4383-afec-afcfb987459a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a348f3ee234ac4fb3","attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- design: Create a design canvas - a multi-artboard visual design published as an Artifact that runs Claude Design's canvas editor (an early preview of Claude Design inside Claude Code). You DRAFT the design as .dc.html artboards laid out on one pan/zoom canvas; where saving is enabled for the user's account they refine every element visually (click-to-select, a properties panel, inline text editing, undo/redo) and Save publishes a new version for everyone, otherwise they get a view-and-export (PNG/PDF) preview of your draft. Good for UI mockups and screen flows, landing pages, marketing and social graphics, and print pieces - posters, flyers, brochures as single-page artboards; memos and reports as one flowing artboard. Use when someone wants a design, mockup, wireframe, UI or screen design, landing page, poster, flyer, brochure, banner, card, one-pager, or any visual layout they would rather tweak by hand than in code. Only for CREATING or re-seeding a canvas; an existing one is edited in its published Artifact.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, or handing the viewer a file to save. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever the user asks for an artifact needing any such runtime behavior.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init\n- security-review","skillCount":18,"isInitial":true,"names":["design","dataviz","artifact-design","artifact-diagramming","artifact-capabilities","update-config","keybindings-help","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","workflow-authoring","claude-in-chrome","run","init","security-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"adb841d3-5ad6-433c-8159-dcbd4296c906","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:41:25.628Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"adb841d3-5ad6-433c-8159-dcbd4296c906","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a348f3ee234ac4fb3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CeZLwS48AngqQ6hxSPgh1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll start by reading all the dependency modules to understand the interfaces and data structures."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5852,"cache_read_input_tokens":16388,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5852,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CeZLwR8oh2PDBWk1Du5P5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"21ebfc40-d563-4281-bed8-a77a2e2f84a1","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:41:28.989Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"21ebfc40-d563-4281-bed8-a77a2e2f84a1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a348f3ee234ac4fb3","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CeZLwS48AngqQ6hxSPgh1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PV6DiwrCuCmtNPpDBcLzyg","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/group.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5852,"cache_read_input_tokens":16388,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5852,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CeZLwR8oh2PDBWk1Du5P5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0e3bd698-3229-4e77-bbec-970b99f491b0","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:41:29.699Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
{"parentUuid":"0e3bd698-3229-4e77-bbec-970b99f491b0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e22f4082-815d-4291-8ec0-471a24337109","agentId":"a348f3ee234ac4fb3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PV6DiwrCuCmtNPpDBcLzyg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Binary icosahedral group 2I: 120 unit quaternions, explicit construction,\n2\tcharacter table, generating pair, and unitary representations for all 9 sectors.\n3\t\n4\tRepresentations R1, R3, R6, R7, R8 are unitarized symmetric powers of the\n5\tSU(2) fundamental. R2 is extracted from V_7 via the Reynolds projector (first\n6\toccurrence of R2 is at level 7). R4 = Sym^2(R2) unitarized. R5 = R1 x R2.\n7\t\n8\tAll constructions match right_translation_check.py and mode_count.py, verified\n9\tat import time.\n10\t\"\"\"\n11\timport numpy as np\n12\timport itertools\n13\tfrom math import factorial\n14\tfrom scipy.linalg import sqrtm\n15\t\n16\tTAU = (1 + np.sqrt(5)) / 2\n17\tTAU_INV = (np.sqrt(5) - 1) / 2\n18\t\n19\t# ---- quaternion arithmetic ----\n20\tdef qmul(a, b):\n21\t    w1, x1, y1, z1 = a\n22\t    w2, x2, y2, z2 = b\n23\t    return np.array([\n24\t        w1*w2 - x1*x2 - y1*y2 - z1*z2,\n25\t        w1*x2 + x1*w2 + y1*z2 - z1*y2,\n26\t        w1*y2 - x1*z2 + y1*w2 + z1*x2,\n27\t        w1*z2 + x1*y2 - y1*x2 + z1*w2,\n28\t    ])\n29\t\n30\tdef quat_to_su2(q):\n31\t    w, x, y, z = q\n32\t    return np.array([[w + 1j*x, y + 1j*z],\n33\t                     [-y + 1j*z, w - 1j*x]], dtype=complex)\n34\t\n35\tdef sym_power(M, n):\n36\t    dim = n + 1\n37\t    out = np.zeros((dim, dim), dtype=complex)\n38\t    for k in range(dim):\n39\t        polyA = np.array([1.0 + 0j])\n40\t        for _ in range(n - k):\n41\t            polyA = np.convolve(polyA, np.array([M[0, 0], M[1, 0]]))\n42\t        polyB = np.array([1.0 + 0j])\n43\t        for _ in range(k):\n44\t            polyB = np.convolve(polyB, np.array([M[0, 1], M[1, 1]]))\n45\t        out[:, k] = np.convolve(polyA, polyB)\n46\t    return out\n47\t\n48\t# ---- group construction (matches right_translation_check.py) ----\n49\tdef _build_icosians():\n50\t    Q = []\n51\t    for i in range(4):\n52\t        for s in (1.0, -1.0):\n53\t            q = np.zeros(4); q[i] = s; Q.append(q)\n54\t    for signs in range(16):\n55\t        Q.append(np.array([0.5 if (signs >> k) & 1 == 0 else -0.5 for k in range(4)]))\n56\t    base = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 1/TAU, TAU]) / 2\n57\t    evens = [p for p in itertools.permutations(range(4))\n58\t             if sum(1 for a in range(4) for b in range(a+1, 4) if p[a] > p[b]) % 2 == 0]\n59\t    for p in evens:\n60\t        for signs in range(8):\n61\t            sb = np.array([1.0, (-1)**(signs & 1), (-1)**((signs >> 1) & 1),\n62\t                           (-1)**((signs >> 2) & 1)])\n63\t            v = base * sb\n64\t            Q.append(np.array([v[p.index(k)] for k in range(4)]))\n65\t    return np.array(Q)\n66\t\n67\tG120 = _build_icosians()\n68\tassert G120.shape == (120, 4)\n69\t\n70\tdef order_of(q):\n71\t    p = q.copy()\n72\t    for k in range(1, 12):\n73\t        if np.abs(p - np.array([1, 0, 0, 0])).sum() < 1e-9:\n74\t            return k\n75\t        p = qmul(p, q)\n76\t    return -1\n77\t\n78\t# ---- verification ----\n79\t_unique_count = len({tuple(np.round(q, 9)) for q in G120})\n80\tassert _unique_count == 120\n81\tassert np.allclose(np.einsum('ij,ij->i', G120, G120), 1.0)\n82\t_order_census = {}\n83\tfor q in G120:\n84\t    o = order_of(q)\n85\t    _order_census[o] = _order_census.get(o, 0) + 1\n86\tassert _order_census == {1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 20, 4: 30, 5: 24, 6: 20, 10: 24}\n87\t\n88\tdef _closure_residual(G):\n89\t    maxres = 0.0\n90\t    for i in range(len(G)):\n91\t        for j in range(len(G)):\n92\t            p = qmul(G[i], G[j])\n93\t            dists = np.sqrt(((G - p)**2).sum(axis=1))\n94\t            maxres = max(maxres, dists.min())\n95\t    return maxres\n96\t\n97\t_cres = _closure_residual(G120)\n98\tassert _cres < 1e-9\n99\t\n100\t# ---- generating pair ----\n101\tdef _find_generators():\n102\t    ord10s = [i for i, q in enumerate(G120) if order_of(q) == 10]\n103\t    ord4s = [i for i, q in enumerate(G120) if order_of(q) == 4]\n104\t    def generate(seed_idx):\n105\t        S = {tuple(np.round(G120[i], 9)) for i in seed_idx}\n106\t        frontier = [G120[i] for i in seed_idx]\n107\t        while frontier:\n108\t            nxt = []\n109\t            for a in frontier:\n110\t                for i in seed_idx:\n111\t                    p = qmul(a, G120[i])\n112\t                    tp = tuple(np.round(p, 9))\n113\t                    if tp not in S:\n114\t                        S.add(tp)\n115\t                        nxt.append(p)\n116\t            frontier = nxt\n117\t        return S\n118\t    for a in ord10s[:3]:\n119\t        for b in ord4s:\n120\t            if len(generate([a, b])) == 120:\n121\t                return [a, b]\n122\t    raise RuntimeError(\"no generating pair found\")\n123\t\n124\tGEN_INDICES = _find_generators()\n125\t\n126\t# ---- conjugacy class snapping ----\n127\tCLASS_ANGLES = np.array([0, np.pi, np.pi/2, np.pi/3, 2*np.pi/3,\n128\t                         np.pi/5, 2*np.pi/5, 3*np.pi/5, 4*np.pi/5])\n129\tCLASS_SIZES = np.array([1, 1, 30, 20, 20, 12, 12, 12, 12])\n130\t\n131\tdef snap_class(q):\n132\t    th = np.arccos(np.clip(q[0], -1, 1))\n133\t    k = int(np.argmin(np.abs(CLASS_ANGLES - th)))\n134\t    assert abs(CLASS_ANGLES[k] - th) < 1e-9, f\"angle {th} off every 2I class\"\n135\t    return k\n136\t\n137\tELEMENT_CLASSES = np.array([snap_class(q) for q in G120])\n138\t\n139\t# ---- character table ----\n140\tGALOIS_CLASS = {5: 7, 7: 5, 6: 8, 8: 6}\n141\t\n142\tdef chiV(n, k):\n143\t    th = CLASS_ANGLES[k]\n144\t    if k == 0:\n145\t        return float(n + 1)\n146\t    if k == 1:\n147\t        return float((n + 1) * (-1)**n)\n148\t    return np.sin((n + 1) * th) / np.sin(th)\n149\t\n150\tIRREP_NAMES = [\"R0\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R3\", \"R4\", \"R5\", \"R6\", \"R7\", \"R8\"]\n151\t\n152\tdef _build_chartab():\n153\t    IRREPS8 = {\n154\t        \"R0\": lambda k: 1.0,\n155\t        \"R1\": lambda k: chiV(1, k),\n156\t        \"R2\": lambda k: chiV(1, GALOIS_CLASS.get(k, k)),\n157\t        \"R3\": lambda k: chiV(2, k),\n158\t        \"R4\": lambda k: chiV(2, GALOIS_CLASS.get(k, k)),\n159\t        \"R6\": lambda k: chiV(3, k),\n160\t        \"R7\": lambda k: chiV(4, k),\n161\t        \"R8\": lambda k: chiV(5, k),\n162\t    }\n163\t    others_vec = {r: np.array([IRREPS8[r](k) for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES]) for r in IRREPS8}\n164\t    import collections\n165\t    csize = collections.Counter(ELEMENT_CLASSES)\n166\t    cent = {k: 120 // csize[k] for k in csize}\n167\t    absq = {k: cent[k] - sum(IRREPS8[r](k)**2 for r in IRREPS8) for k in csize}\n168\t    amb = [k for k in sorted(csize) if absq[k] > 0.5 and k != 0]\n169\t    hits = []\n170\t    for bits in range(2 ** len(amb)):\n171\t        row = {k: float(np.sqrt(max(absq[k], 0.0))) for k in csize}\n172\t        for j, k in enumerate(amb):\n173\t            if (bits >> j) & 1:\n174\t                row[k] = -row[k]\n175\t        vec = np.array([row[k] for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES])\n176\t        if (all(abs(np.mean(vec * others_vec[r])) < 1e-9 for r in IRREPS8)\n177\t                and abs(np.mean(vec * vec) - 1.0) < 1e-9):\n178\t            hits.append(vec)\n179\t    assert len(hits) == 1, f\"R5 sign solution not unique: {len(hits)}\"\n180\t    tab = {r: others_vec[r] for r in IRREPS8}\n181\t    tab[\"R5\"] = hits[0]\n182\t    return tab\n183\t\n184\tCHARTAB = _build_chartab()\n185\t\n186\t_orth = np.array([[np.mean(CHARTAB[a] * CHARTAB[b]) for b in IRREP_NAMES]\n187\t                  for a in IRREP_NAMES])\n188\tassert np.abs(_orth - np.eye(9)).max() < 1e-9\n189\t\n190\tDIMS = {name: int(round(CHARTAB[name][0])) for name in IRREP_NAMES}\n191\tD_RHO = {\"R0\": 0, \"R1\": 1, \"R3\": 2, \"R6\": 3, \"R7\": 4, \"R8\": 5, \"R4\": 6, \"R5\": 6, \"R2\": 7}\n192\t\n193\tdef multiplicity(rho, n):\n194\t    return int(round(np.mean(np.array([chiV(n, k) for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES]) * CHARTAB[rho])))\n195\t\n196\t# ---- unitarization of symmetric powers ----\n197\tdef _sym_gram(n):\n198\t    \"\"\"Diagonal Gram for Sym^n in monomial basis: G_kk = k!(n-k)!/n!\"\"\"\n199\t    fn = factorial(n)\n200\t    return np.array([factorial(k) * factorial(n - k) / fn for k in range(n + 1)])\n201\t\n202\tdef pi_n_mono(n, q):\n203\t    if n == 0:\n204\t        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n205\t    return sym_power(quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float)), n)\n206\t\n207\tdef _pi_n_cg_recurrence(n, U):\n208\t    \"\"\"Sym^n in unitarized (weight) basis via CG recurrence. Stable at all n.\"\"\"\n209\t    D = U.copy()\n210\t    for step in range(1, n):\n211\t        dim_old = step + 1\n212\t        dim_new = step + 2\n213\t        D_pad = np.zeros((dim_new, dim_new), dtype=complex)\n214\t        D_pad[:dim_old, :dim_old] = D\n215\t        D_pm = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n216\t        D_pm[:, 1:] = D_pad[:, :dim_new - 1]\n217\t        D_mp = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n218\t        D_mp[1:, :] = D_pad[:dim_new - 1, :]\n219\t        D_mm = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n220\t        D_mm[1:, 1:] = D_pad[:dim_new - 1, :dim_new - 1]\n221\t        i_arr = np.arange(dim_new, dtype=float)\n222\t        cg_op = np.sqrt(np.clip((dim_old - i_arr) / dim_old, 0, None))\n223\t        cg_om = np.sqrt(np.clip(i_arr / dim_old, 0, None))\n224\t        CG_pp = cg_op[:, None] * cg_op[None, :]\n225\t        CG_pm = cg_op[:, None] * cg_om[None, :]\n226\t        CG_mp = cg_om[:, None] * cg_op[None, :]\n227\t        CG_mm = cg_om[:, None] * cg_om[None, :]\n228\t        D = (CG_pp * D_pad * U[0, 0] + CG_pm * D_pm * U[0, 1] +\n229\t             CG_mp * D_mp * U[1, 0] + CG_mm * D_mm * U[1, 1])\n230\t    return D\n231\t\n232\tdef pi_n_unitary(n, q):\n233\t    if n == 0:\n234\t        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n235\t    U = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n236\t    if n <= 40:\n237\t        g = _sym_gram(n)\n238\t        Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n239\t        Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n240\t        return Dh @ sym_power(U, n) @ Dih\n241\t    return _pi_n_cg_recurrence(n, U)\n242\t\n243\t# ---- extract R2 from V_7 via Reynolds projector ----\n244\tdef _build_R2_matrices():\n245\t    \"\"\"Extract R2 (dim 2) from V_7|_{2I} using the character-weighted Reynolds projector.\"\"\"\n246\t    d = 8  # dim V_7\n247\t    all_V7 = np.zeros((120, d, d), dtype=complex)\n248\t    for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n249\t        all_V7[i] = pi_n_unitary(7, q)\n250\t    chi_R2 = CHARTAB[\"R2\"]\n251\t    P = np.zeros((d, d), dtype=complex)\n252\t    for i in range(120):\n253\t        P += chi_R2[i].conj() * all_V7[i]\n254\t    P *= 2.0 / 120.0\n255\t    eigvals, eigvecs = np.linalg.eigh(P)\n256\t    mask = eigvals > 0.5\n257\t    assert mask.sum() == 2, f\"expected rank 2, got {mask.sum()}\"\n258\t    U = eigvecs[:, mask]  # 8x2\n259\t    R2_mats = np.zeros((120, 2, 2), dtype=complex)\n260\t    for i in range(120):\n261\t        R2_mats[i] = U.conj().T @ all_V7[i] @ U\n262\t    return R2_mats\n263\t\n264\t_R2_ALL = _build_R2_matrices()\n265\t\n266\t# ---- build R4 = Sym^2(R2) ----\n267\tdef _sym2_of_mat(M):\n268\t    \"\"\"Sym^2 of a 2x2 matrix in the monomial basis {u^2, uv, v^2}.\"\"\"\n269\t    return sym_power(M, 2)\n270\t\n271\tdef _build_R4_matrices():\n272\t    \"\"\"R4 = unitarized Sym^2(R2).\n273\t    R2 is already unitary (det 1), so Sym^2(R2) uses the same Gram as Sym^2(SU2).\"\"\"\n274\t    g = _sym_gram(2)\n275\t    Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n276\t    Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n277\t    R4_mats = np.zeros((120, 3, 3), dtype=complex)\n278\t    for i in range(120):\n279\t        R4_mats[i] = Dh @ _sym2_of_mat(_R2_ALL[i]) @ Dih\n280\t    return R4_mats\n281\t\n282\t_R4_ALL = _build_R4_matrices()\n283\t\n284\t# ---- build R5 = R1 tensor R2 ----\n285\tdef _build_R5_matrices():\n286\t    R5_mats = np.zeros((120, 4, 4), dtype=complex)\n287\t    for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n288\t        R1 = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n289\t        R5_mats[i] = np.kron(R1, _R2_ALL[i])\n290\t    return R5_mats\n291\t\n292\t_R5_ALL = _build_R5_matrices()\n293\t\n294\t# ---- precompute all rep matrices ----\n295\tdef _precompute_all():\n296\t    \"\"\"Precompute all 120 matrices for every sector.\"\"\"\n297\t    all_reps = {}\n298\t    for rho in IRREP_NAMES:\n299\t        d = DIMS[rho]\n300\t        mats = np.zeros((120, d, d), dtype=complex)\n301\t        for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n302\t            mats[i] = _rep_rho_single(rho, i)\n303\t        all_reps[rho] = mats\n304\t    return all_reps\n305\t\n306\tdef _rep_rho_single(rho, idx):\n307\t    q = G120[idx]\n308\t    if rho == \"R0\":\n309\t        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n310\t    elif rho == \"R1\":\n311\t        return quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n312\t    elif rho == \"R2\":\n313\t        return _R2_ALL[idx]\n314\t    elif rho == \"R3\":\n315\t        return pi_n_unitary(2, q)\n316\t    elif rho == \"R4\":\n317\t        return _R4_ALL[idx]\n318\t    elif rho == \"R5\":\n319\t        return _R5_ALL[idx]\n320\t    elif rho == \"R6\":\n321\t        return pi_n_unitary(3, q)\n322\t    elif rho == \"R7\":\n323\t        return pi_n_unitary(4, q)\n324\t    elif rho == \"R8\":\n325\t        return pi_n_unitary(5, q)\n326\t    raise ValueError(f\"unknown sector: {rho}\")\n327\t\n328\tALL_REPS = _precompute_all()\n329\t\n330\tdef rep_rho(rho, idx):\n331\t    \"\"\"Unitary representation matrix of G120[idx] in sector rho.\"\"\"\n332\t    return ALL_REPS[rho][idx]\n333\t\n334\tdef rep_rho_quat(rho, q):\n335\t    \"\"\"Unitary representation matrix of quaternion q in sector rho.\n336\t    q must be in G120 (found by nearest-neighbor lookup).\"\"\"\n337\t    dists = np.sqrt(((G120 - q)**2).sum(axis=1))\n338\t    idx = int(np.argmin(dists))\n339\t    assert dists[idx] < 1e-9, f\"quaternion not in 2I\"\n340\t    return ALL_REPS[rho][idx]\n341\t\n342\t# ---- verification ----\n343\tdef _verify_reps():\n344\t    for rho in IRREP_NAMES:\n345\t        d = DIMS[rho]\n346\t        mats = ALL_REPS[rho]\n347\t        # unitarity\n348\t        for i in range(120):\n349\t            M = mats[i]\n350\t            assert M.shape == (d, d)\n351\t            assert np.abs(M @ M.conj().T - np.eye(d)).max() < 1e-9, \\\n352\t                f\"{rho}[{i}]: not unitary\"\n353\t        # character\n354\t        for i in range(120):\n355\t            tr = np.trace(mats[i]).real\n356\t            expected = CHARTAB[rho][i]\n357\t            assert abs(tr - expected) < 1e-7, \\\n358\t                f\"{rho}[{i}]: trace {tr} != {expected}\"\n359\t        # homomorphism on full group\n360\t        for _ in range(50):\n361\t            a, b = np.random.randint(0, 120, 2)\n362\t            p = qmul(G120[a], G120[b])\n363\t            pidx = int(np.argmin(np.sqrt(((G120 - p)**2).sum(axis=1))))\n364\t            err = np.abs(mats[a] @ mats[b] - mats[pidx]).max()\n365\t            assert err < 1e-9, f\"{rho}: homomorphism error {err}\"\n366\t        # rho(-1) = +-I\n367\t        minus1_idx = int(np.argmin(np.sqrt(((G120 - np.array([-1, 0, 0, 0]))**2).sum(axis=1))))\n368\t        Mm = mats[minus1_idx]\n369\t        assert np.abs(np.abs(Mm) - np.eye(d)).max() < 1e-9, f\"{rho}: rho(-1) not +-I\"\n370\t\n371\t_verify_reps()\n372\t"}]},"uuid":"1cabddbe-fea4-4a5e-a892-0d0234ac2f2e","timestamp":"2026-08-30T17:41:29.707Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/group.py","content":"\"\"\"Binary icosahedral group 2I: 120 unit quaternions, explicit construction,\ncharacter table, generating pair, and unitary representations for all 9 sectors.\n\nRepresentations R1, R3, R6, R7, R8 are unitarized symmetric powers of the\nSU(2) fundamental. R2 is extracted from V_7 via the Reynolds projector (first\noccurrence of R2 is at level 7). R4 = Sym^2(R2) unitarized. R5 = R1 x R2.\n\nAll constructions match right_translation_check.py and mode_count.py, verified\nat import time.\n\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nimport itertools\nfrom math import factorial\nfrom scipy.linalg import sqrtm\n\nTAU = (1 + np.sqrt(5)) / 2\nTAU_INV = (np.sqrt(5) - 1) / 2\n\n# ---- quaternion arithmetic ----\ndef qmul(a, b):\n    w1, x1, y1, z1 = a\n    w2, x2, y2, z2 = b\n    return np.array([\n        w1*w2 - x1*x2 - y1*y2 - z1*z2,\n        w1*x2 + x1*w2 + y1*z2 - z1*y2,\n        w1*y2 - x1*z2 + y1*w2 + z1*x2,\n        w1*z2 + x1*y2 - y1*x2 + z1*w2,\n    ])\n\ndef quat_to_su2(q):\n    w, x, y, z = q\n    return np.array([[w + 1j*x, y + 1j*z],\n                     [-y + 1j*z, w - 1j*x]], dtype=complex)\n\ndef sym_power(M, n):\n    dim = n + 1\n    out = np.zeros((dim, dim), dtype=complex)\n    for k in range(dim):\n        polyA = np.array([1.0 + 0j])\n        for _ in range(n - k):\n            polyA = np.convolve(polyA, np.array([M[0, 0], M[1, 0]]))\n        polyB = np.array([1.0 + 0j])\n        for _ in range(k):\n            polyB = np.convolve(polyB, np.array([M[0, 1], M[1, 1]]))\n        out[:, k] = np.convolve(polyA, polyB)\n    return out\n\n# ---- group construction (matches right_translation_check.py) ----\ndef _build_icosians():\n    Q = []\n    for i in range(4):\n        for s in (1.0, -1.0):\n            q = np.zeros(4); q[i] = s; Q.append(q)\n    for signs in range(16):\n        Q.append(np.array([0.5 if (signs >> k) & 1 == 0 else -0.5 for k in range(4)]))\n    base = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 1/TAU, TAU]) / 2\n    evens = [p for p in itertools.permutations(range(4))\n             if sum(1 for a in range(4) for b in range(a+1, 4) if p[a] > p[b]) % 2 == 0]\n    for p in evens:\n        for signs in range(8):\n            sb = np.array([1.0, (-1)**(signs & 1), (-1)**((signs >> 1) & 1),\n                           (-1)**((signs >> 2) & 1)])\n            v = base * sb\n            Q.append(np.array([v[p.index(k)] for k in range(4)]))\n    return np.array(Q)\n\nG120 = _build_icosians()\nassert G120.shape == (120, 4)\n\ndef order_of(q):\n    p = q.copy()\n    for k in range(1, 12):\n        if np.abs(p - np.array([1, 0, 0, 0])).sum() < 1e-9:\n            return k\n        p = qmul(p, q)\n    return -1\n\n# ---- verification ----\n_unique_count = len({tuple(np.round(q, 9)) for q in G120})\nassert _unique_count == 120\nassert np.allclose(np.einsum('ij,ij->i', G120, G120), 1.0)\n_order_census = {}\nfor q in G120:\n    o = order_of(q)\n    _order_census[o] = _order_census.get(o, 0) + 1\nassert _order_census == {1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 20, 4: 30, 5: 24, 6: 20, 10: 24}\n\ndef _closure_residual(G):\n    maxres = 0.0\n    for i in range(len(G)):\n        for j in range(len(G)):\n            p = qmul(G[i], G[j])\n            dists = np.sqrt(((G - p)**2).sum(axis=1))\n            maxres = max(maxres, dists.min())\n    return maxres\n\n_cres = _closure_residual(G120)\nassert _cres < 1e-9\n\n# ---- generating pair ----\ndef _find_generators():\n    ord10s = [i for i, q in enumerate(G120) if order_of(q) == 10]\n    ord4s = [i for i, q in enumerate(G120) if order_of(q) == 4]\n    def generate(seed_idx):\n        S = {tuple(np.round(G120[i], 9)) for i in seed_idx}\n        frontier = [G120[i] for i in seed_idx]\n        while frontier:\n            nxt = []\n            for a in frontier:\n                for i in seed_idx:\n                    p = qmul(a, G120[i])\n                    tp = tuple(np.round(p, 9))\n                    if tp not in S:\n                        S.add(tp)\n                        nxt.append(p)\n            frontier = nxt\n        return S\n    for a in ord10s[:3]:\n        for b in ord4s:\n            if len(generate([a, b])) == 120:\n                return [a, b]\n    raise RuntimeError(\"no generating pair found\")\n\nGEN_INDICES = _find_generators()\n\n# ---- conjugacy class snapping ----\nCLASS_ANGLES = np.array([0, np.pi, np.pi/2, np.pi/3, 2*np.pi/3,\n                         np.pi/5, 2*np.pi/5, 3*np.pi/5, 4*np.pi/5])\nCLASS_SIZES = np.array([1, 1, 30, 20, 20, 12, 12, 12, 12])\n\ndef snap_class(q):\n    th = np.arccos(np.clip(q[0], -1, 1))\n    k = int(np.argmin(np.abs(CLASS_ANGLES - th)))\n    assert abs(CLASS_ANGLES[k] - th) < 1e-9, f\"angle {th} off every 2I class\"\n    return k\n\nELEMENT_CLASSES = np.array([snap_class(q) for q in G120])\n\n# ---- character table ----\nGALOIS_CLASS = {5: 7, 7: 5, 6: 8, 8: 6}\n\ndef chiV(n, k):\n    th = CLASS_ANGLES[k]\n    if k == 0:\n        return float(n + 1)\n    if k == 1:\n        return float((n + 1) * (-1)**n)\n    return np.sin((n + 1) * th) / np.sin(th)\n\nIRREP_NAMES = [\"R0\", \"R1\", \"R2\", \"R3\", \"R4\", \"R5\", \"R6\", \"R7\", \"R8\"]\n\ndef _build_chartab():\n    IRREPS8 = {\n        \"R0\": lambda k: 1.0,\n        \"R1\": lambda k: chiV(1, k),\n        \"R2\": lambda k: chiV(1, GALOIS_CLASS.get(k, k)),\n        \"R3\": lambda k: chiV(2, k),\n        \"R4\": lambda k: chiV(2, GALOIS_CLASS.get(k, k)),\n        \"R6\": lambda k: chiV(3, k),\n        \"R7\": lambda k: chiV(4, k),\n        \"R8\": lambda k: chiV(5, k),\n    }\n    others_vec = {r: np.array([IRREPS8[r](k) for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES]) for r in IRREPS8}\n    import collections\n    csize = collections.Counter(ELEMENT_CLASSES)\n    cent = {k: 120 // csize[k] for k in csize}\n    absq = {k: cent[k] - sum(IRREPS8[r](k)**2 for r in IRREPS8) for k in csize}\n    amb = [k for k in sorted(csize) if absq[k] > 0.5 and k != 0]\n    hits = []\n    for bits in range(2 ** len(amb)):\n        row = {k: float(np.sqrt(max(absq[k], 0.0))) for k in csize}\n        for j, k in enumerate(amb):\n            if (bits >> j) & 1:\n                row[k] = -row[k]\n        vec = np.array([row[k] for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES])\n        if (all(abs(np.mean(vec * others_vec[r])) < 1e-9 for r in IRREPS8)\n                and abs(np.mean(vec * vec) - 1.0) < 1e-9):\n            hits.append(vec)\n    assert len(hits) == 1, f\"R5 sign solution not unique: {len(hits)}\"\n    tab = {r: others_vec[r] for r in IRREPS8}\n    tab[\"R5\"] = hits[0]\n    return tab\n\nCHARTAB = _build_chartab()\n\n_orth = np.array([[np.mean(CHARTAB[a] * CHARTAB[b]) for b in IRREP_NAMES]\n                  for a in IRREP_NAMES])\nassert np.abs(_orth - np.eye(9)).max() < 1e-9\n\nDIMS = {name: int(round(CHARTAB[name][0])) for name in IRREP_NAMES}\nD_RHO = {\"R0\": 0, \"R1\": 1, \"R3\": 2, \"R6\": 3, \"R7\": 4, \"R8\": 5, \"R4\": 6, \"R5\": 6, \"R2\": 7}\n\ndef multiplicity(rho, n):\n    return int(round(np.mean(np.array([chiV(n, k) for k in ELEMENT_CLASSES]) * CHARTAB[rho])))\n\n# ---- unitarization of symmetric powers ----\ndef _sym_gram(n):\n    \"\"\"Diagonal Gram for Sym^n in monomial basis: G_kk = k!(n-k)!/n!\"\"\"\n    fn = factorial(n)\n    return np.array([factorial(k) * factorial(n - k) / fn for k in range(n + 1)])\n\ndef pi_n_mono(n, q):\n    if n == 0:\n        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n    return sym_power(quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float)), n)\n\ndef _pi_n_cg_recurrence(n, U):\n    \"\"\"Sym^n in unitarized (weight) basis via CG recurrence. Stable at all n.\"\"\"\n    D = U.copy()\n    for step in range(1, n):\n        dim_old = step + 1\n        dim_new = step + 2\n        D_pad = np.zeros((dim_new, dim_new), dtype=complex)\n        D_pad[:dim_old, :dim_old] = D\n        D_pm = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n        D_pm[:, 1:] = D_pad[:, :dim_new - 1]\n        D_mp = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n        D_mp[1:, :] = D_pad[:dim_new - 1, :]\n        D_mm = np.zeros_like(D_pad)\n        D_mm[1:, 1:] = D_pad[:dim_new - 1, :dim_new - 1]\n        i_arr = np.arange(dim_new, dtype=float)\n        cg_op = np.sqrt(np.clip((dim_old - i_arr) / dim_old, 0, None))\n        cg_om = np.sqrt(np.clip(i_arr / dim_old, 0, None))\n        CG_pp = cg_op[:, None] * cg_op[None, :]\n        CG_pm = cg_op[:, None] * cg_om[None, :]\n        CG_mp = cg_om[:, None] * cg_op[None, :]\n        CG_mm = cg_om[:, None] * cg_om[None, :]\n        D = (CG_pp * D_pad * U[0, 0] + CG_pm * D_pm * U[0, 1] +\n             CG_mp * D_mp * U[1, 0] + CG_mm * D_mm * U[1, 1])\n    return D\n\ndef pi_n_unitary(n, q):\n    if n == 0:\n        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n    U = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n    if n <= 40:\n        g = _sym_gram(n)\n        Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n        Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n        return Dh @ sym_power(U, n) @ Dih\n    return _pi_n_cg_recurrence(n, U)\n\n# ---- extract R2 from V_7 via Reynolds projector ----\ndef _build_R2_matrices():\n    \"\"\"Extract R2 (dim 2) from V_7|_{2I} using the character-weighted Reynolds projector.\"\"\"\n    d = 8  # dim V_7\n    all_V7 = np.zeros((120, d, d), dtype=complex)\n    for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n        all_V7[i] = pi_n_unitary(7, q)\n    chi_R2 = CHARTAB[\"R2\"]\n    P = np.zeros((d, d), dtype=complex)\n    for i in range(120):\n        P += chi_R2[i].conj() * all_V7[i]\n    P *= 2.0 / 120.0\n    eigvals, eigvecs = np.linalg.eigh(P)\n    mask = eigvals > 0.5\n    assert mask.sum() == 2, f\"expected rank 2, got {mask.sum()}\"\n    U = eigvecs[:, mask]  # 8x2\n    R2_mats = np.zeros((120, 2, 2), dtype=complex)\n    for i in range(120):\n        R2_mats[i] = U.conj().T @ all_V7[i] @ U\n    return R2_mats\n\n_R2_ALL = _build_R2_matrices()\n\n# ---- build R4 = Sym^2(R2) ----\ndef _sym2_of_mat(M):\n    \"\"\"Sym^2 of a 2x2 matrix in the monomial basis {u^2, uv, v^2}.\"\"\"\n    return sym_power(M, 2)\n\ndef _build_R4_matrices():\n    \"\"\"R4 = unitarized Sym^2(R2).\n    R2 is already unitary (det 1), so Sym^2(R2) uses the same Gram as Sym^2(SU2).\"\"\"\n    g = _sym_gram(2)\n    Dh = np.diag(np.sqrt(g))\n    Dih = np.diag(1.0 / np.sqrt(g))\n    R4_mats = np.zeros((120, 3, 3), dtype=complex)\n    for i in range(120):\n        R4_mats[i] = Dh @ _sym2_of_mat(_R2_ALL[i]) @ Dih\n    return R4_mats\n\n_R4_ALL = _build_R4_matrices()\n\n# ---- build R5 = R1 tensor R2 ----\ndef _build_R5_matrices():\n    R5_mats = np.zeros((120, 4, 4), dtype=complex)\n    for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n        R1 = quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n        R5_mats[i] = np.kron(R1, _R2_ALL[i])\n    return R5_mats\n\n_R5_ALL = _build_R5_matrices()\n\n# ---- precompute all rep matrices ----\ndef _precompute_all():\n    \"\"\"Precompute all 120 matrices for every sector.\"\"\"\n    all_reps = {}\n    for rho in IRREP_NAMES:\n        d = DIMS[rho]\n        mats = np.zeros((120, d, d), dtype=complex)\n        for i, q in enumerate(G120):\n            mats[i] = _rep_rho_single(rho, i)\n        all_reps[rho] = mats\n    return all_reps\n\ndef _rep_rho_single(rho, idx):\n    q = G120[idx]\n    if rho == \"R0\":\n        return np.array([[1.0 + 0j]])\n    elif rho == \"R1\":\n        return quat_to_su2(np.asarray(q, float))\n    elif rho == \"R2\":\n        return _R2_ALL[idx]\n    elif rho == \"R3\":\n        return pi_n_unitary(2, q)\n    elif rho == \"R4\":\n        return _R4_ALL[idx]\n    elif rho == \"R5\":\n        return _R5_ALL[idx]\n    elif rho == \"R6\":\n        return pi_n_unitary(3, q)\n    elif rho == \"R7\":\n        return pi_n_unitary(4, q)\n    elif rho == \"R8\":\n        return pi_n_unitary(5, q)\n    raise ValueError(f\"unknown sector: {rho}\")\n\nALL_REPS = _precompute_all()\n\ndef rep_rho(rho, idx):\n    \"\"\"Unitary representation matrix of G120[idx] in sector rho.\"\"\"\n    return ALL_REPS[rho][idx]\n\ndef rep_rho_quat(rho, q):\n    \"\"\"Unitary representation matrix of quaternion q in sector rho.\n    q must be in G120 (found by nearest-neighbor lookup).\"\"\"\n    dists = np.sqrt(((G120 - q)**2).sum(axis=1))\n    idx = int(np.argmin(dists))\n    assert dists[idx] < 1e-9, f\"quaternion not in 2I\"\n    return ALL_REPS[rho][idx]\n\n# ---- verification ----\ndef _verify_reps():\n    for rho in IRREP_NAMES:\n        d = DIMS[rho]\n        mats = ALL_REPS[rho]\n        # unitarity\n        for i in range(120):\n            M = mats[i]\n            assert M.shape == (d, d)\n            assert np.abs(M @ M.conj().T - np.eye(d)).max() < 1e-9, \\\n                f\"{rho}[{i}]: not unitary\"\n        # character\n        for i in range(120):\n            tr = np.trace(mats[i]).real\n            expected = CHARTAB[rho][i]\n            assert abs(tr - expected) < 1e-7, \\\n                f\"{rho}[{i}]: trace {tr} != {expected}\"\n        # homomorphism on full group\n        for _ in range(50):\n            a, b = np.random.randint(0, 120, 2)\n            p = qmul(G120[a], G120[b])\n            pidx = int(np.argmin(np.sqrt(((G120 - p)**2).sum(axis=1))))\n            err = np.abs(mats[a] @ mats[b] - mats[pidx]).max()\n            assert err < 1e-9, f\"{rho}: homomorphism error {err}\"\n        # rho(-1) = +-I\n        minus1_idx = int(np.argmin(np.sqrt(((G120 - np.array([-1, 0, 0, 0]))**2).sum(axis=1))))\n        Mm = mats[minus1_idx]\n        assert np.abs(np.abs(Mm) - np.eye(d)).max() < 1e-9, f\"{rho}: rho(-1) not +-I\"\n\n_verify_reps()\n","numLines":372,"startLine":1,"totalLines":372}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0e3bd698-3229-4e77-bbec-970b99f491b0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD","sessionId":"df404d7f-30da-41ec-8c3e-12ef3f92c53b","version":"2.1.251","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-orbiting-pelican"}
